  "mathematics": {
    "scope": "Complete original Chapter21 source read163 lines, one author input. Earlier Chapter1,8,9,11,19 inputs retain their recorded current-cycle scopes; no new independent external certification of cited classical equation or product/descent inputs is made.",
    "fresh_checks": [
      "The scalar Hopf forms and fiber integral keep the Chapter19 normalization alpha=pi*sigma, zeta^2=0, integral_fiber zeta=2, and dV=(1/2)alpha^(n-1) wedge zeta with total mass one.",
      "The homogeneous section metric |f(z)|^2|z|^(-2k) is invariant under every nonzero complex scalar and identifies L_k with the pulled-back O(k) metric; powers correspond to degree km.",
      "The Chapter1 proper connected-fiber submersion descent result applies to u in PSH(X_n,k alpha), including k=0, and gives a unique genuine quasi-psh base potential in PSH(k sigma).",
      "Unweighted section spaces on both sides contain the same degree-km homogeneous polynomials by Chapter19. Fiber integration against the probability volume identifies weighted norms as possibly infinite integrals of a nonnegative Borel function.",
      "Finiteness of these norms characterizes the multiplier-ideal spaces, so pullback is an isometry; corresponding orthonormal bases give exact K and off-pole B identities, with the zero-space convention retained.",
      "Surjectivity of pi makes boundedness of a base weighted density equivalent to boundedness of its pullback; multiplication is respected, identifying the bounded graded section algebras.",
      "The notation pi*nu wedge zeta is defined by fiber integration rather than an undefined pullback of measures; on smooth top forms it agrees with the usual exterior product.",
      "If v is sigma-psh then pi*(sigma+dd^c v)+zeta is positive. Conversely wedging the positive pulled-back-background current with semipositive zeta and pushing forward gives exactly 2(sigma+dd^c v), since zeta^2=0 and each fiber integral is two.",
      "For bounded v, the Chapter1 bounded-product submersion identity applies; only one zeta factor survives the n-dimensional expansion, with binomial coefficient n because the base has dimension n-1.",
      "For general v<=0, positive sigma makes v_j=max(v,-j) bounded sigma-psh and u_j=pi*v_j the matching truncation. The common indicator is pulled back from the base, and monotone nonnegative fiber integration passes to the extended measures, including zero mass on poles.",
      "Each fiber has zeta-mass two, so the total Hermitian mass is 2n times the base extended mass. Restricted truncation mass is at most the full bounded closed product of mass one, and bounded v attains equality.",
      "The n-dimensional extended measure in background k alpha vanishes: every bounded truncation current pulls back from an (n-1)-dimensional base and its nth power is zero, also for k=0.",
      "For any fiber-constant omega-psh potential, local sections give a quasi-psh base potential and the proved converse gives sigma positivity. Its mass is therefore at most 2n; the known unbounded Hopf-threefold masses must vary along fibers.",
      "The descended equation with dV=(1/2)pi*sigma^(n-1) wedge zeta is equivalent to the base equation with factor c/(2n); pushing forward gives the same factor, rather than a missing fiber integral.",
      "Closed Stokes on the bounded base fixes c=2n/integral f sigma^(n-1). Positive integral is required, and p>1 is retained for the continuous existence and uniqueness theorem.",
      "The earlier Chapter11 Guan-Li theorem applies because sigma is closed, and uniqueness is confined to bounded base potentials, hence the descended bounded class. The earlier Chapter9 smooth theorem applies only to smooth positive densities.",
      "The real-lambda exponential equation statement is an exact reduction of equations with right side c exp(lambda u)(f circ pi); it makes no additional existence or uniqueness claim for arbitrary lambda.",
      "For smooth projective torus-invariant v and real k>0, logarithmic variables give F(x+t1)=F(x)+kt. The local Hermitian matrix of dd^c F is a positive constant times (F_ij/(z_i conjugate(z_j))), congruent to the real Hessian.",
      "Differentiating translation gives sum F_i=k and Hessian times1=0. Bounded v gives uniform error from k log(sum exp x_i); because n>=2, every coordinate-line function is bounded above toward minus infinity, forcing its convex derivative nonnegative.",
      "Positive definiteness of the base form makes the logarithmic Hessian positive definite transverse to the scalar direction. Each coordinate-line derivative is strictly increasing and globally nonnegative, so every F_i is strictly positive and the gradient lies in the relative interior.",
      "For every p_i>0 with sum p_i=k, the homogeneous expression sum p_i(max x-x_i) is positive on nonzero vectors in H=sum x_i=0 and has a positive minimum on its unit sphere. The uniform logarithmic error gives coercivity of F-p dot x on H.",
      "Strict convexity and coercivity give one minimizer on H; its critical gradient differs from p by a scalar multiple of1, whose coordinate sum is zero, so that multiple vanishes. This gives surjectivity onto the relative interior and injectivity on the quotient."
    ],
